GC discriminator: does anything rely on a string-data word to keep its string alive?  (integ-wh m12, ledger S2.26)
2026-10-24, GNU 31.1 as the reference, every NeLisp run capped at 8 GiB of address space, each job one bin/heavy-run.sh unit.
Variants (one dynamic build each, 643 s and 649 s): poison-pure = prototype 6 tree + the poison patch (a swept string keeps only the size word; its other data bytes are 0xDD),
poison-hyb = prototype 7 tree + the same patch.  The pure scan drops every sdata word; the hybrid still lets a sdata word read from a pinned block mark its owner.
Binaries: nelisp-dyn-poisondrop v1.5.0-2234-g156a8708d, nelisp-dyn-poisonhyb v1.5.0-2235-g0f967560a (branches eval/integ-wh-20261022-m12-poison-drop and -poison-hyb).

1. The 11 groups of target/final-verify/checks.sh, private log directories (LC_ALL=C.UTF-8):
   poison-pure: GATE-COUNT: 11 cases, 0 failures (141 standalone checks, known-red as before: standalone-maphash-sparse-check.el)
   poison-hyb: GATE-COUNT: 11 cases, 0 failures (141 standalone checks, known-red as before: standalone-maphash-sparse-check.el)
   An earlier run showed two failures on pure (standalone-symbols-check and standalone-syntax-rt-chartab-check, "GNU output mismatch") and none on hybrid.  It does not reproduce:
   the runs above and 68 more per variant (section 2) pass.  The symptom, those two names with that message, is reproduced by two concurrent test/perf/standalone-checks.sh
   runs of just those two tests on the pure binary that share the default log directory target/standalone-checks/ (the first run reports both failures, the second none);
   with a private STANDALONE_CHECK_LOGDIR neither fails.  The earlier run executed in the batch worktree, whose own runs of the suite write the same directory, so that is
   the likely cause; nothing else has been found.

